CONCEPTUAL DEFINITIONS 

Phase space
For a system of particles, phase space is the six-dimensional space defined by position and velocity coordinates

(x, v) = (x, y, z, vx, vy, vz)

Each particle corresponds to a single point in this space, and the full dynamical state of the system is represented by the distribution of all particles in phase space.

In the code, phase space is explicitly stored as the first six columns of the state array and is used directly when computing coarse-grained phase-space density and entropy.

Liouville’s theorem
Liouville’s theorem states that for a Hamiltonian system evolving under conservative forces, the fine-grained phase-space density is conserved along trajectories:

df/dt = 0

This holds even for strongly time-dependent or chaotic gravitational systems.

In the code, Newtonian gravity and a symplectic leapfrog integrator approximate Hamiltonian evolution. Any observed entropy growth does not violate Liouville’s theorem and arises from coarse-graining and finite numerical resolution.

Phase-space irreversibility
Phase-space irreversibility refers to the practical impossibility of reconstructing the initial phase-space distribution from a later evolved state, despite the underlying equations of motion being time-reversible.

Formally, although the microscopic equations satisfy:

(x, v, t) → (x, −v, −t)

the coarse-grained phase-space distribution does not return to its original configuration.

In the code, irreversibility is diagnosed by the monotonic increase of coarse-grained phase-space entropy, driven by phase mixing, chaotic orbital divergence, and numerical coarse-graining.

Disk heating
Disk heating is the process by which a galactic disk increases its vertical thickness and velocity dispersion due to gravitational perturbations.

Vertical thickening is quantified by:

h_z = sqrt(<z^2>)

Velocity dispersion is quantified by:

σ^2 = Var(vx, vy, vz)

In the code, both quantities are computed for disk particles and tracked over time to diagnose tidal heating during the MW–M31 merger.

Angular momentum evolution
Angular momentum evolution describes how angular momentum is redistributed within and between galactic components during dynamical interactions.

The z-component of angular momentum is defined as:

L_z = Σ m (x vy − y vx)

In the code, L_z is computed per galactic component and tracked over time to study tidal torques, orbital decay, and internal angular momentum transfer.

HST-constrained merger setup
An HST-constrained merger setup initializes the Milky Way–Andromeda system using observationally inferred relative positions and velocities derived from Hubble Space Telescope proper-motion measurements.

In the code, center-of-mass positions and velocities are computed for each galaxy, and corrective shifts are applied so that:

Δr ≈ 780 kpc
Δv ≈ (−109, 20, 0) km/s

This anchors the simulation to real observational constraints rather than arbitrary initial conditions.

Entropy evolution
Entropy evolution refers to the time evolution of coarse-grained phase-space entropy, which measures the degree of mixing and disorder in the system.

Entropy is defined as:

S = − Σ_i P_i ln(P_i)

where P_i is the normalized phase-space density in bin i.

In the code, entropy is computed from six-dimensional phase-space histograms and tracked across simulation snapshots.

Angular momentum budget
The angular momentum budget is the accounting of angular momentum content within each galactic component as a function of time.

In the code, the angular momentum budget is computed separately for the Milky Way disk and Andromeda disk, enabling analysis of conservation, transfer, and merger-driven redistribution.

ADDITIONAL IMPLIED DEFINITIONS

Coarse-graining
The process of dividing continuous phase space into finite bins in order to compute statistical quantities such as entropy.

Phase mixing
The spreading of nearby phase-space trajectories due to differences in orbital frequencies, leading to apparent irreversibility.

Symplectic integration
A numerical integration method that preserves phase-space structure and long-term stability for Hamiltonian systems.

Snapshot
A discrete record of the full system state at a specific simulation time.

Collisionless dynamics
An approximation in which direct particle–particle collisions are neglected and evolution is governed by smooth gravitational forces.
